<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jonang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jonangpa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jonang is a school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> that took its name from the monastery of Jonang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a>, founded in the late 13th century. Its great teacher Dolpopa Sherab Gyaltsen (1292–1361) developed the zhentong ('emptiness of other') doctrine, and the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranatha">Taranatha</a> (1575–1634) made it prominent again under the patronage of the Tsangpa kings. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> victory of 1642 its monasteries in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> were converted, but it survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amdo">Amdo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jonang.jpg" alt="Jonang" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jonang Foundation · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolpopa%27s_Great_Stupa_at_Jomonang,_Tibet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
